<Project>
  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Pinned to the workspace baseline. Expecto is test-only. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="FSharp.Core" Version="10.1.300" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Expecto" Version="9.0.4" />
    <!-- Fable client tier — consumed only by samples/, never by a shipping package. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Fable.Core" Version="5.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Feliz" Version="3.3.3" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Fuaran.UI.Renderer" Version="0.78.1" />
    <!-- The substrate, pinned DIRECTLY (Phase 1896). The core packages reference
         no UI-tier package, so what they use of the substrate they name
         themselves: the JSON value and canonical form (Wire), the columnar
         table (Column), the evaluator a server read runs (DataFrame), the node
         witness and hash (Tree), and the stream witness and signing sink
         (OpStream). Until Phase 1896 every one of these arrived only
         transitively, through the UI tier.

         Pinned at 0.21.0 — exactly the substrate the UI-tier pin above was
         BUILT on — and deliberately NOT at the current Core-Compute release.
         This repository's own UI adapter shares this restore graph (it is
         compiled beside the core, and under Fable it is compiled FROM SOURCE
         against these very versions), and no published UI-tier release
         satisfies both halves of a raise: the pinned UI tier's sources do not
         compile against the 0.34.0 evaluator (its `Limit` takes slots, and its
         clock grain captures the `Date` / `Timestamp` case names the tier's
         resolver uses), while every UI-tier release whose sources do compile
         against it re-encodes the tree the program wire corpus pins. The
         measurements, the compile errors and the route forward are recorded
         in docs/dataframe-0.34-surface-diff.md: the evaluator raise travels
         WITH the UI adapter's own raise, as one act. Every version here is one
         the PUBLIC restore path serves exactly — `tools/check-pins.ps1`
         refuses anything else. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Fuaran.Core.Wire" Version="0.21.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Fuaran.Core.Column" Version="0.21.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Fuaran.Core.DataFrame" Version="0.21.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Fuaran.Core.Tree" Version="0.21.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Fuaran.Core.OpStream" Version="0.21.0" />
    <!-- The UI tier — the first host instantiation consumes its types by
         PackageReference off the local feed / nuget.org (DECISIONS.md D4). The
         dependency runs ONE WAY: nothing in the UI tier references back, which
         is what keeps this repo buildable from a cold clone against published
         packages alone (D5). One property per producing repo, so a deliberate
         lag behind a breaking change is a visible decision rather than drift.

         EVERY version here must be one the declared restore path serves EXACTLY.
         A pin naming a version nothing publishes is not caught by the build: it
         is NU1603, a warning, after which the nearest higher version is
         substituted silently. This family sat at 0.25.0 — a version published
         nowhere, servable only by a machine whose local feed happened to hold a
         locally-packed copy — and floated a minor ahead in every fresh clone,
         where the NU1603 output aborted the Fable project crack and presented as
         assembly-reference errors naming nothing. `tools/check-pins.ps1` runs
         before the build and now refuses both shapes.

         The family is pinned at 0.78.1 — the newest version every one of these
         eight ids is served at by the PUBLIC restore path, checked against the
         registry's own index rather than against a local feed. That distinction
         is the point: this package set publishes, so a consumer restoring it
         reaches nuget.org and nothing else, and a pin only a maintainer's machine
         can resolve would make the published package unrestorable for everyone
         else while building perfectly here. -->
    <!-- The published sequence for this family is not contiguous: many versions
         between the released ones were cut and never published, so a gap in the
         sequence is not a gap in the pin. Nothing below is held back any more —
         the pin names the producer's newest PUBLISHED version, so no
         deliberate-lag marker belongs beside any id here. A marker naming a
         version the pin has left does not merely go stale: it silences a real
         finding while appearing to explain one, which is why clearing the
         markers is part of a raise rather than tidy-up after it.
         A tag is not a publish — check the registry index, not the tag list.

         Do not write that marker token itself into prose like this. The sweep
         quotes the nearest preceding comment carrying it, so a paragraph that
         merely NAMES the token is quoted in place of the marker that follows,
         and the attribution a reader is shown is a fragment of the wrong
         sentence. Observed here before this wording. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Fuaran.UI" Version="0.78.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Fuaran.UI.Ops" Version="0.78.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Fuaran.UI.Ops.Abstractions" Version="0.78.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Fuaran.UI.OpStream.Abstractions" Version="0.78.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Fuaran.UI.OpStream.Replay" Version="0.78.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Fuaran.UI.Renderer.Core" Version="0.78.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Fuaran.UI.ServerDriven" Version="0.78.1" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
